{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the origins of facebook with a sharp, rapid-fire script and a hauntingly atmospheric score. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie transforms what could have been a dry corporate origin story into a gripping psychological drama about ambition, betrayal, and the cost of innovation. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, capturing his brilliance, social awkwardness, and emotional detachment with chilling precision. his portrayal is not a caricature but a nuanced study of a young man whose genius is matched only by his inability to connect with others on a human level.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally strong. andrew garfield brings heart and vulnerability to eduardo saverin, zuckerberg’s loyal friend turned rival, while jesse plemons and jordan belfort (as sean parker) provide contrasting energies—plemons as the grounded, morally conflicted co-founder, and parker as the charismatic, chaotic force that pulls zuckerberg further into the world of tech excess. the screenplay is a tour de force: dialogue crackles with wit and tension, and sorkin’s trademark rapid monologues never feel forced—they propel the narrative forward with relentless momentum.\n\nfincher’s direction is immaculate. every frame feels deliberate, from the cold blue tones of harvard’s ivy-covered halls to the neon-lit clubs of silicon valley. the use of slow motion during pivotal moments, like the opening breakup scene, adds emotional weight without melodrama. trent reznor and atticus ross’s score is integral to the film’s mood—a pulsing, electronic soundscape that mirrors the digital age’s heartbeat, simultaneously beautiful and isolating.\n\nthe film’s structure, told through two legal depositions framing the story, cleverly mirrors the dual nature of zuckerberg’s legacy: a revolutionary innovator and a cold-hearted opportunist. the courtroom scenes are not just procedural—they’re philosophical battlegrounds, questioning ownership, friendship, and the ethics of creation in the internet era.\n\nwhile some may argue the film takes liberties with historical accuracy, its truth lies not in facts but in emotional resonance. it captures the zeitgeist of a generation that grew up connected yet profoundly alone. the social network isn’t just about facebook—it’s about how technology reshapes human relationships, and how power, once seized, can never be returned. it’s tense, intelligent, and unforgettable. a modern classic.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0003810394846368581}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4176788032054901}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.37157994508743286}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0009815642843022943}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.0058195204474031925}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00013195598148740828}, {"token": " del", "logprob": -0.9510456919670105}, {"token": "ves", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.007622679229825735}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.1920922133867862e-06}, {"token": " origins", "logprob": -0.67808997631073}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.731181277544238e-05}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.0002889215829782188}, {"token": " with", "logprob": -0.8339745998382568}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -1.6908057928085327}, {"token": " sharp", "logprob": -0.33529767394065857}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.27631500363349915}, {"token": " rapid", "logprob": -2.712292194366455}, {"token": "-fire", "logprob": -0.0008205384365282953}, {"token": " script", "logprob": -0.08479230105876923}, {"token": " an